<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meghalaya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meghalaya is a state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, formed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in 1972, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shillong">Shillong</a>. Its hills are home mainly to the Khasi, Jaintia and Garo peoples, who follow matrilineal descent, and include some of the wettest places on earth, such as Mawsynram and Sohra. Its traditional Khasi chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Syiems</a>, keep customary roles alongside the state government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meghalaya.jpg" alt="Meghalaya" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madhumita Das · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawki_River,_Meghalaya,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Meghalaya is one of the Seven Sister States of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It covers about 22,429 square kilometres and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> to the south and west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> to the north and east. The state is mountainous, with valleys and highland plateaus, and rests mainly on Archean rock that holds deposits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, limestone, uranium and sillimanite. Its name means the abode of clouds, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> megha and alaya, and was given by Shiba P. Chatterjee, a geography professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>.</p><p>The southern Khasi Hills record an average of 12,000 mm of rain a year, and about 70 per cent of the state is forested. Some of the forest survives as sacred groves, small pockets of ancient forest kept by communities for centuries for religious reasons and reserved for ritual. British authorities nicknamed the region the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> of the East.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived in Meghalaya since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period. Neolithic sites have been found in the high Khasi Hills and the Garo Hills, where shifting cultivation known as jhum is still practised. The limited archaeology suggests ancient settlement, and Ian Glover has proposed that north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was the most favourable single area for the origin of domesticated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Folk tradition ties jhum to rice: one legend has the god of wind shake seeds from a celestial tree, which a bird sowed.</p><p>Khasis, Jaintias and Garos each kept traditional political institutions that, according to the account, have existed for hundreds of years and worked at village, clan and state level. Meghalaya itself was formed on 21 January 1972 by carving the united Khasi Hills and Jaintia Hills and the Garo Hills out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. Its Autonomous District Councils exist under the Sixth Schedule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a>, which was added on the recommendation of a subcommittee led by Gopinath Bordoloi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shillong">Shillong</a> is the capital. The Khasis are the largest group in the population, followed by the Garos and the Jaintias, who follow a matrilineal system in which descent and inheritance pass through women, and the youngest daughter inherits the property and cares for aged parents. About 75 per cent of people are Christian. Meghalaya has 12 districts and a Legislative Assembly of 60 members.</p><p>Living root bridges, made by training the aerial roots of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> tree Ficus elastica, are found in the south, built by both Khasis and Jaintias. In July 2018 the International Commission on Stratigraphy named the Meghalayan age, the latest stage of the Holocene, after a stalagmite in Mawmluh cave that records a climate event around 2250 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meghalaya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jaintia kingdom grew from chiefdoms of the Pnar (Synteng) people of the eastern <b>Meghalaya</b> plateau, who extended their rule into the plains north of Sylhet.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaintia-kingdom">Jaintia Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Jirang was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <b>Meghalaya</b> plateau, lying towards the Assam plains in the present Ri Bhoi area.</blockquote><a href="/state/jirang-state">Jirang State</a></li><li><blockquote>It is now part of <b>Meghalaya</b>, where the Nongkrem festival continues.</blockquote><a href="/state/khyrim-state">Khyrim State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Langrin was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <b>Meghalaya</b> plateau; its origins are known only from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/state/langrin-state">Langrin State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Maharam was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <b>Meghalaya</b> plateau; its origins are known only from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/state/maharam-state">Maharam State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hima Mawiang was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <b>Meghalaya</b> plateau; its origins are known only from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</blockquote><a href="/state/mawiang-state">Mawiang State</a></li></ul></section>
